# UI Components Guidelines

This project uses **Nuxt 4**, **Nuxt UI 4**, and **Tailwind CSS 4** for building user interfaces.

## Technology Stack

- **Nuxt 4**: Full-stack Vue framework
- **Nuxt UI 4**: Component library built on top of Tailwind CSS
- **Tailwind CSS 4**: Utility-first CSS framework (comes bundled with Nuxt UI 4)

## Documentation References

When working with UI components, always refer to the latest official documentation:

- **Nuxt UI 4 Components**: https://ui.nuxt.com/docs/components
- **Nuxt 4 Guide**: https://nuxt.com/docs/4.x/guide

## Best Practices

### 1. Use Nuxt UI Components First

Always prefer Nuxt UI components over custom implementations when available.

<!-- ✅ Good: Use Nuxt UI components -->
<template>
  <UButton color="primary" variant="solid">Click me</UButton>
  <UCard>
    <template #header>Title</template>
    Content here
  </UCard>
</template>

<!-- ❌ Bad: Custom button when UButton exists -->
<template>
  <button class="custom-button">Click me</button>
</template>### 2. Component Props and API

Refer to the Nuxt UI documentation for component props, slots, and events:

- Check available props: https://ui.nuxt.com/docs/components/button#api
- Use TypeScript types for type safety
- Follow component-specific patterns from documentation

### 3. Styling with Tailwind CSS 4

- Use Tailwind utility classes for styling
- Leverage Nuxt UI's theme system for consistent colors and spacing
- Use component-scoped styles (`<style scoped>`) for component-specific styling
- Define global CSS variables in `app/assets/css/main.css` for theme customization

<!-- ✅ Good: Use Tailwind utilities -->
<template>
  <div class="flex items-center gap-4 p-6 bg-white dark:bg-gray-900">
    <UButton class="px-6 py-3">Button</UButton>
  </div>
</template>

<!-- ❌ Bad: Inline styles or custom CSS when Tailwind can handle it -->
<template>
  <div style="display: flex; padding: 24px;">
    <button style="padding: 12px 24px;">Button</button>
  </div>
</template>### 4. Component Composition

- Break down complex UIs into smaller, reusable components
- Use Nuxt UI components as building blocks
- Create wrapper components when you need to extend Nuxt UI components with custom logic

<!-- ✅ Good: Composable component using Nuxt UI -->
<template>
  <UButton
    v-bind="$attrs"
    :class="buttonClass"
    @click="handleClick"
  >
    <slot />
  </UButton>
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
// Custom logic while leveraging Nuxt UI
</script>### 5. Dark Mode Support

- Always consider dark mode when styling components
- Use Nuxt UI's built-in dark mode support
- Test components in both light and dark modes

<!-- ✅ Good: Dark mode aware -->
<template>
  <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-900 text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
    Content
  </div>
</template>### 6. Responsive Design

- Use Tailwind's responsive utilities (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`)
- Test components on different screen sizes
- Leverage Nuxt UI's responsive components when available

### 7. Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Ensure proper ARIA attributes when needed
- Nuxt UI components include accessibility features by default

## Common Nuxt UI Components

### Layout Components

- `UApp`: Root app wrapper
- `UHeader`: Header component
- `UFooter`: Footer component
- `UContainer`: Content container
- `UMain`: Main content area

### Form Components

- `UButton`: Button element
- `UInput`: Text input
- `UCard`: Card container
- `UForm`: Form with validation
- `UFormField`: Form field wrapper

### Data Display

- `UTable`: Table component
- `UCard`: Card display
- `UBadge`: Badge component
- `UAvatar`: Avatar image

### Navigation

- `UTabs`: Tab navigation
- `UBreadcrumb`: Breadcrumb navigation
- `UPagination`: Pagination controls

Refer to https://ui.nuxt.com/docs/components for the complete list of available components.

## When to Create Custom Components

Create custom components when:

1. **Extending Nuxt UI components**: Wrap Nuxt UI components with additional logic
2. **Domain-specific components**: Create components specific to your application domain
3. **Complex compositions**: Combine multiple Nuxt UI components into reusable patterns

Always check if a Nuxt UI component can fulfill your needs before creating a custom component.

